=== HINT 1: Open volume ===
HANDLE vol = CreateFileA("\\\\.\\C:",
    GENERIC_READ, FILE_SHARE_READ | FILE_SHARE_WRITE,
    NULL, OPEN_EXISTING, FILE_FLAG_NO_BUFFERING, NULL);

=== HINT 2: IOCTL call ===
BOOL ok = DeviceIoControl(
    vol,                         // device handle
    FSCTL_GET_NTFS_VOLUME_DATA,  // control code
    NULL, 0,                     // input buffer (none for this IOCTL)
    &nvd, sizeof(nvd),           // output buffer
    &returned,                   // bytes returned
    NULL);                       // not overlapped

=== HINT 3: Close ===
CloseHandle(vol);

=== KEY INSIGHT ===
DeviceIoControl is the universal user→kernel gateway.
The IOCTL code encodes the driver (device type), the function number,
the required access level, and how buffers are passed (method):
  METHOD_BUFFERED:  kernel copies input/output through a kernel buffer (safest)
  METHOD_IN_DIRECT: input copied, output uses user MDL
  METHOD_OUT_DIRECT: output uses user MDL
  METHOD_NEITHER:   raw user pointers passed to driver (dangerous)

To write your own kernel driver IOCTL, define a CTL_CODE:
  #define MY_IOCTL CTL_CODE(FILE_DEVICE_UNKNOWN, 0x800, METHOD_BUFFERED, FILE_ANY_ACCESS)
